--TEST--
`Core\Router::urlAbsolute` throws on a route it resolved when the unit has no configured origin, where `Core\Router::url` answers — ADR 0102 § 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The pair asserted on both sides: the *same* name, resolved by the same
// § 4 lookup, answered by one member and refused by the other. What separates
// them is only ADR 0102 § 6's origin, and a CLI run has none — § 6 puts the
// origin on the mount and there is no mount off the command line to expand,
// which is the one place this refusal can be provoked without a server.
//
// The refusal matters because the alternative is not an error: `urlAbsolute`
// could answer `//posts/1` with an empty authority, or read `Host` off the
// request. § 6 forbids the second outright — an absolute link's origin is
// configured per mount and never derived from a header — so the member has to
// throw rather than answer something a caller would paste into an email.
class Posts {
    // `rule:attributes/access-is-a-required-sibling`: `#[Access]` is a required sibling, and
    // `Core\Audience::Public` is § 1a's case for a route that is genuinely
    // open.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/posts/{id}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Posts::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(uint $id): string { return "post " . $id; }
}

// The side that answers. Nothing about the route is unusual, which is what
// makes the other side about the origin and not about the lookup.
echo "relative=", Core\Router::url("Posts::show", ["id" => 1]), "\n";

// The side that refuses. The message is frozen whole rather than counted: it
// is the one place a program is told *where* to configure the origin, and a
// refusal that stopped naming `nvs.toml` would still pass a case asserting
// only that something threw.
// Bound to a variable first rather than echoed inline: `echo` writes each
// argument as it reaches it, so an inline call would have printed the label
// before the throw and this case would be pinning the order of two writes.
try {
    var $absolute = Core\Router::urlAbsolute("Posts::show", ["id" => 1]);
    echo "absolute=", $absolute, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "threw: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// And the refusal is the origin's, not the name's: the same unknown name is
// refused by both members (which
// `router-url-and-url-absolute-refuse-the-same-unknown-names.nvst` pins), so
// a `urlAbsolute` that had simply stopped resolving would print the same
// first word here. The path inside the message above is the one `url`
// answered, which is what says the lookup succeeded and the origin is what
// was missing.
--EXPECT--
relative=/posts/1
threw: Core\Router::urlAbsolute(): no origin is configured for this unit, so `/posts/1` has no absolute form. ADR 0102 § 6 refuses to derive one from a request header, so give `nvs.toml` an `[[app]] origin`
